BBC News,Western Europe,en,"US has floated Ukraine ceding Donbas for Nato membership, Zelensky says","US has floated Ukraine ceding Donbas for Nato membership, Zelensky says","US has floated Ukraine ceding Donbas for Nato membership, Zelensky says The US has not confirmed the Ukrainian president's comments - a US official told the BBC ""many ideas continue to be discussed"". US negotiators have touted the idea of Ukraine withdrawing its remaining forces from the eastern Donbas region in exchange for Nato membership, President Volodymyr Zelensky has said. The US has not confirmed Zelensky's comments, and a US official told the BBC ""many ideas continue to be discussed but any final deal will have to be agreed to by both parties"". The Ukrainian leader's comments came after US envoys met Russian President Vladimir Putin and Zelensky as part of US efforts to end the war between Russia and Ukraine. Kyiv has long sought membership of the military alliance, but this has previously been opposed by the US and others while Russia has said another Nato member on its border is unacceptable. Russia launched a full-scale invasion of Ukraine in February 2022. It controls most of the industrial region of Donbas and Zelensky has consistently said Ukraine is unwilling to give up sovereign territory. ""The Americans raised a question: 'What if Ukraine joins Nato? Would Ukraine withdraw from Donbas?' This was the first time this had been raised,"" Zelensky said in remarks released by his office on Saturday. He went on to say that if he received a specific proposal that contained security guarantees, no constraints on Ukraine's military and funds to rebuild the country, he would put it to a referendum. US envoys Jared Kushner and Steve Witkoff travelled to the Irish capital, Dublin, on Thursday to meet Zelensky, after a round of talks with Putin in Moscow. Comments by b","Sat, 14 Nov 2026 22:37:25 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cwvg2l312ylqo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-11-15 03:26:06
BBC News,Western Europe,en,"Copper wire upgrade caused nationwide flight suspension, US transport secretary says","Copper wire upgrade caused nationwide flight suspension, US transport secretary says","Copper wire upgrade caused nationwide flight suspension, US transport secretary says The US government is trying to find out whether the chaos was caused by ""incompetence"" or faulty equipment. A nationwide US ground stop that caused thousands of flight cancellations was caused by an issue following a copper wire upgrade, Transport Secretary Sean Duffy said. Duffy told a news conference that the government was trying to find out whether the problem was caused by ""incompetence"" on the part of the company doing the upgrades, or faulty equipment. The US government does not believe it was as a result of a cyber-attack, although an investigation is still under way, Duffy said. Friday's suspension was the longest such incident since the 11 September 2001 attacks on the US and the disruption is expected to last for days. Maintenance was being carried out to switch from copper wire to fibre by a contractor called L3Harris, a technology provider company, Duffy said. The US has ""an incredibly old telecommunications system"", he added. ""It was such an old wire that it was being discontinued,"" he said. The transport secretary explained that a router that was being worked on had started flooding other air traffic control systems and created communication issues with planes which led the government to shut down the airspace for safety reasons. Bryan Bedford, who heads the Federal Aviation Administration (FAA), told the same news conference: ""We see zero evidence of anything nefarious, and zero evidence of anything AI related."" On Saturday L3Harris said it had completed over 70% of the work to modernise communications infrastructure before the shutdown. ""We are supporting the FAA i","Sat, 14 Nov 2026 19:12:25 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c6wy9lynq4qno?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-11-15 03:26:06
BBC News,Western Europe,en,Pope Leo speaks of community 'suffering' on visit to remote Andes town,Pope Leo speaks of community 'suffering' on visit to remote Andes town,"Pope Leo speaks of community 'suffering' on visit to remote Andes town Santa Cruz de Succhabamba is part of the Diocese of Chiclayo, where the pope served as bishop from 2015 to 2023. On a visit to an isolated Andean village in his former diocese, Pope Leo XIV has spoken about the ""suffering of those who are forced to leave their homeland in search of a better future"", which he said had left communities under threat. About 6,000 people live in Santa Cruz de Succhabamba, which is located in a remote part of the Andes mountains in northern Peru, but the Pope's visit drew a crowd of about 20,000, according to the Vatican. At an outdoor Mass, Pope Leo spoke of ""declining birth rates that are emptying villages,"" and the unequal distribution of the land's resources. Over 40% of the population in the Cajamarca region, where the town is located, live in poverty - the highest figure in Peru, according to official data. Cajamarca has lost the highest number of people to internal migration, with 729,000 people born there now living elsewhere in Peru. In his sermon, Pope Leo thanked those who had made the journey to him and emphasised the importance of never losing heart, despite life's difficulties. Santa Cruz is within the Diocese of Chiclayo, where Pope Leo served as bishop from 2015 to 2023. During his tenure, he visited Santa Cruz several times a year to minister to the town's rural population. On Saturday he returned by helicopter, where he was greeted by singing and cheering. He later did a tour in a popemobile, where he waved to people who had travelled from across the region to see him. Speaking at a local parish on Saturday, the Pope said he felt ""joy"" at be","Sun, 15 Nov 2026 02:49:45 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cr4gnz0lv2vlo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-11-15 03:26:06
BBC News,Western Europe,en,Judge temporarily blocks Trump from closing Washington DC golf course for renovation,Judge temporarily blocks Trump from closing Washington DC golf course for renovation,"Judge temporarily blocks Trump from closing Washington DC golf course for renovation It is the latest development in a months-long challenge to the president's plans for East Potomac Golf Links. A federal judge has temporarily blocked the US government's closure of a historic golf course and park in Washington DC. US District Judge Ana Reyes has been overseeing a months-long challenge to the president's plans for East Potomac Golf Links, which he said would be closed from 15 November for extensive renovations. Reyes has said Donald Trump cannot close the park or its golf course, but added that his administration was not required to actively manage either. Trump, an avid golfer, has said his plans to upgrade the 106-year-old course will create ""one of the Best and Most Spectacular Golf Courses anywhere in the World"". The BBC has contacted the White House for comment. Declaring the current course ""Trash Island"", he announced in October it would be renovated over 15 months to create a space that ""will be open to the Public while, at the same time, designed to the Highest Level of Golf and able to hold World Class Tournaments"" - despite the ongoing litigation. In a 30-page decision issued on Saturday, Reyes said the Trump administration's justifications for the proposed course closures were pretextual, and that the government violated at least two federal laws ""to meet an arbitrary deadline."" ""The Government proposes to close a historic park, daily enjoyed by Washingtonians of all stripes, indefinitely,"" Reyes wrote. She said the closure ""runs counter to the public interest in utilising national parkland"", adding that the government had not ""plausibly identified any harm","Sun, 15 Nov 2026 03:21:04 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c6j9mn7l14y0o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-11-15 03:26:06
BBC News,Western Europe,en,Italy fines Meta €805m over accessing users' photos,Italy fines Meta €805m over accessing users' photos,"Italy fines Meta €805m over accessing users' photos The social media giant is accused of not informing 20 million users it had access to their photos. Italian regulators have fined social media giant Meta €805m (£670m; $887m) for accessing Android users' photos without their specific consent. Italy's Data Protection Authority said Meta did not properly inform about 20 million smartphone users that it had access to their photos and videos via a feature of the Facebook app. It has given Meta 60 days to disable the feature by default and seek users' consent separately. The fine is among the largest an Italian authority has ever imposed on a tech giant. A Meta spokesperson told the BBC that it planned to appeal. The charge is related to a feature which suggests photos and videos from a user's phone gallery as ideas for posts. Facebook requested access to users' photo galleries through a single app-wide authorisation. According to the Italian regulator, a preceding notice did not make clear that this would also allow Facebook to generate suggestions based on photos in their gallery. The regulator said it took into account ""the high number of people affected"" and the fact it is ""still ongoing for those who have not updated the app and for those who have already given their consent to the system"". It also cited ""Meta's lack of co-operation"". Meta told the BBC: ""We strongly disagree with the [regulator's] assessment of the facts, its application of the law, and the disproportionate level of the fine. ""This is a straightforward feature which helps people post photos and videos to Facebook. People must give permission on their device","Sat, 14 Nov 2026 18:28:06 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cwn83gxemk35o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-11-15 03:26:06
BBC News,Western Europe,en,'Can you believe it?': SpaceX catches part of its Starship rocket on return to Earth,'Can you believe it?': SpaceX catches part of its Starship rocket on return to Earth,"'Can you believe it?': SpaceX catches part of its Starship rocket on return to Earth SpaceX has performed the historic catch using mechanical arms nicknamed ""chopsticks"". SpaceX has successfully caught the top part of its huge Starship rocket on its return to Earth for the first time. ""Can you believe it?,"" a commentator said as large crowds cheered at the launch site in Texas. The company had hoped to catch both the bottom and upper stages of the rocket using mechanical arms - nicknamed chopsticks - on its launch tower. But the first catch of the rocket's booster was aborted a few minutes after the launch on Friday. The booster was diverted to the ocean, bursting into flames just above the sea. The top part, known as the Ship, returned to Earth a few minutes behind schedule at around 15:20 local time (21:20 GMT) on Saturday, in the first-ever successful catch attempt. Read more on this story:SpaceX successfully catches top of huge Starship rocket for first time","Sat, 14 Nov 2026 22:09:40 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/videos/cwlym2gy3y3eo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-11-15 03:26:06
BBC News,Western Europe,en,Philippines wins first Eurovision Song Contest Asia,Philippines wins first Eurovision Song Contest Asia,"Philippines wins first Eurovision Song Contest Asia Front-runner Carmelle emerges victorious with her women's empowerment anthem Sobra-Sobra. The Philippines have won the very first Eurovision Song Contest Asia, hosted in Bangkok and with 11 countries vying for the title. Nineteen-year-old singer Carmelle was always a front runner with her women's empowerment anthem Sobra-Sobra, complete with its catchy rap section. She earned 211 points in total - 93 from the jury and 118 from the public vote - ahead of Thailand's MANG x DAOU in second place with 170 points. There was a joyous atmosphere at the One Bangkok Arena as the crowd cheered the contestants and celebrated the popular winner. Carmelle, the youngest contestant in the competition, was an obvious favourite with the juries and the public despite strong showings from several nations. Other notable acts included MANG x DAOU, whose emotive duet Aroma elicited huge cheers from the home crowd. Bangladeshi beatboxers Beatmosphere eschewed the traditional backing track, instead using their voices to replicate the sounds of drums and basslines. And Laos's Thinla performed her crowd-pleasing song Bae in a traditional Lao hilltribe outfit. However, the Laotian song had a unintentional second outing after the music video was played over an apparent technical issue during the broadcast - meaning it was the last song the audience heard before the vote. The voting system worked much the same as the European Eurovison, with the public vote accounting for 50% of the result and the other 50% coming from the 11 national juries - with additional points awarded from a rest of the ","Sat, 14 Nov 2026 18:17:58 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cqrlgnx9dx6eo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-11-15 03:26:06
BBC News,Western Europe,en,Sotheby's auction of $180m Van Gogh challenged over claims it was 'sold under duress',Sotheby's auction of $180m Van Gogh challenged over claims it was 'sold under duress',"Sotheby's auction of $180m Van Gogh challenged over claims it was 'sold under duress' Sotheby's says it will work with the heirs of a family who had previously owned the artwork to try and reach a solution. Auction house Sotheby's says it is working to resolve a dispute over the intended sale of a painting by Vincent van Gogh. Châtaigniers en Fleurs, which carries an estimate of $180m (£136m), is due to go under the hammer in New York on Wednesday. But representatives acting for heirs of the Fischer family - earlier German owners of the work - say it may have been sold under duress during the rise of the Nazis. Sotheby's say its own ""extensive research"" has not established this, but say they will work with the family to try and reach a solution. ""Sotheby's takes provenance and restitution matters extremely seriously and has undertaken extensive research into the ownership history of Châtaigniers en fleurs,"" Sotheby's told the BBC in a statement. ""We are aware of the concerns recently raised by representatives of the Fischer heirs and have been in dialogue with them regarding the painting's history. ""While our research to date has not established that the painting was sold under duress, we recognize the importance of engaging thoughtfully and constructively with the family and are committed to working toward a mutually satisfactory resolution."" The painting - whose name translates as Blossoming Chestnut Trees - is being sold as part of a set called the Blaquier-Arrieta Collection. Sotheby's website details the provenance of the oil painting and how it has researched the artwork changing hands since 1890. It says Samuel Fischer - a famous German publisher - acquired it 1909 before","Sat, 14 Nov 2026 18:00:10 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cmwy05gg75lvo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-11-15 03:26:06
BBC News,Western Europe,en,"Trump, Putin and Xi plan historic joint meeting in China next week, White House says","Trump, Putin and Xi plan historic joint meeting in China next week, White House says","Trump, Putin and Xi plan historic joint meeting in China next week, White House says Trump will discuss the ""reduction of nuclear weapons"" during the unprecedented talks, a spokeswoman says. Donald Trump, Vladimir Putin and Xi Jinping are planning to meet on the sidelines of a summit in China next week, the White House has said. The historic meeting between the respective leaders of the US, Russia and China will take place as Trump attends the Asia-Pacific Economic Co-operation (Apec) summit in the southern city of Shenzhen, spokeswoman Olivia Wales told reporters. ""The president has made it clear that, among other topics, he will discuss the reduction of nuclear weapons in the world, whose proliferation President Trump believes is very dangerous,"" she said. It is understood Iran may also be discussed during the meeting of the world's biggest nuclear powers, but not Ukraine. Russia has confirmed the meeting. China has yet to react. No agreement was expected to follow the talks, an unnamed, senior US official said, according to news agencies AP and Reuters. Trump will arrive in Shenzhen late on Wednesday for the two-day summit between the 21 member states. He will also take part in bilateral talks with Xi on Thursday - their fourth meeting since the US president returned to office in 2025. ""President Trump will conclude his visit to China with a trilateral meeting between the president of the United States, the president of the People's Republic of China, and the president of the Russian Federation,"" Wales said. She said Trump's trip, which will also include attending an Association of South-east Asian Nations (Asean) summit in the Philippines on Tuesday, would ""s","Sat, 14 Nov 2026 15:53:55 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/ckz74jzpy18do?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-11-15 03:26:06
BBC News,Western Europe,en,Arrested generals and a threatened VP: President's son fuels Ugandan succession fever,Arrested generals and a threatened VP: President's son fuels Ugandan succession fever,"Arrested generals and a threatened VP: President's son fuels Ugandan succession fever The 52-year-old general’s provocative posts and military threats have fuelled rumours of a dynastic succession. In Uganda's military, even senior generals are finding that their rank offers little protection from the wrath of the country's powerful army chief. Over the past 15 months, at least a dozen generals and senior officers have been arrested under Gen Muhoozi Kainerugaba, who is also the son of President Yoweri Museveni, in a crackdown that has unsettled the military's top ranks. The arrests come amid growing political tensions over who will succeed Museveni, who has ruled Uganda for four decades. Gen Kainerugaba, 52, is widely seen as a potential successor, although his ambitions appear to be facing resistance within the ruling party. This week, he threatened Vice-President Jessica Alupo with dismissal and possible arrest, while suggesting he should take her position. The ruling National Resistance Movement (NRM) has, however, rejected reports that its senior leadership had rejected a proposal to back Gen Kainerugaba as the next president, describing the claims as ""completely false"". This has all fuelled speculation that the arrests are part of an effort to sideline military officers opposed to the general's presidential ambitions. Others see the arrests as evidence of an anti-corruption drive in which senior commanders are held to account. At the centre of the tension are two powerful powerful institutions: the military, where Gen Kainerugaba wields considerable influence, and the NRM, where questions about his political ambitions appear to be dividing senior figures. A recent","Sat, 14 Nov 2026 00:03:52 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cmlydy0xw45wo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-11-15 03:26:06
BBC News,Western Europe,en,Nigeria cautions against travel via Ethiopia's main airport due to altitude,Nigeria cautions against travel via Ethiopia's main airport due to altitude,"Nigeria cautions against travel via Ethiopia's main airport due to altitude The foreign ministry in Abuja says 6 Nigerians with underlying health problems have died this year while transiting through Addis Ababa. Nigeria's government has cautioned its citizens, in particular those with medical conditions, against travelling via Ethiopia's main airport due to its altitude. The foreign ministry in Abuja said 16 Nigerians with underlying health problems had died this year while transiting through Addis Ababa. The city sits at an altitude of 2,334m (7,657ft) above sea level, making it one of the highest capitals in the world. Bole International Airport is a major hub for travel between Africa, the Middle East and Asia, serving millions of passengers a year. Nigeria said those with cardiovascular and respiratory conditions were at an increased risk. The Nigerian foreign ministry did not disclose the identities of the travellers it said had died, nor the cause of death. It urged anyone with a health condition exacerbated by high altitudes to ""exercise caution"" when planning a journey via Addis Ababa and ""obtain appropriate medical guidance"" before doing so. The ministry stressed that it did not want to discourage Nigerians from travelling through the Ethiopian capital. Visiting an area at a high altitude can be dangerous for people with heart and lung diseases, as the air is thinner and so less oxygen can get into the bloodstream, experts say. This means the heart has to work harder to get enough oxygen to different parts of the body, which can sometimes lead to heart failure among those with existing conditions, according to the American Heart Association. The lack of oxygen at high altitu","Sat, 14 Nov 2026 15:42:54 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cmz6qjpdy2wqo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-11-15 03:26:07
BBC News,Western Europe,en,Hungarian authorities uncover $20m during probe of Orbán-era minister,Hungarian authorities uncover $20m during probe of Orbán-era minister,"Hungarian authorities uncover $20m during probe of Orbán-era minister Prime Minister Péter Magyar promised a sweeping anti-corruption drive after ousting Orbán in April. Nearly $20m (£14.8m) in cash has been found hidden in farm buildings and buried in forests as part of an investigation into a former minister of Viktor Orbán, authorities have said. Miklós Seszták, who served as minister of national development for four of the ex-prime minister's 16-year rule, is accused of accepting bribes as part of a scheme involving the state transport company. He has denied any wrongdoing. Hungary's new Prime Minister Péter Magyar unseated Orbán in a landslide election in April on the promise of launching a sweeping anti-corruption drive. On Friday, following the two-day search for the cash - found in secluded locations across the country - Magyar described it as the end of the ""mafia"". Investigators found wads of banknotes in safes, buried in woodlands and hidden in agricultural and residential buildings and warehouses, as part of the probe into Seszták, the National Asset Recovery and Protection Office (NVVH) said in a statement. The cash amounted to ""5,071,632,000 forints, €2,658,350 and $1,257,100"", it added, bringing the combined value to $19.5m. It has been deposited into the Hungarian State Treasury, the body said. The NVVH was established to investigate allegations of corruption during the Orbán rule, which Magyar has dubbed ""Operation Purgatory"". Magyar promised Hungarians ""regime change"" during the election earlier this year and has set about undoing many Orbán-era policies since entering office. Allegations of corruption and cronyism have long","Sat, 14 Nov 2026 10:42:20 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/ckjwj03w4w0qo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-11-15 03:26:07
BBC News,Western Europe,en,'We'll get home one day' - US travellers impacted by nationwide ground stop,'We'll get home one day' - US travellers impacted by nationwide ground stop,'We'll get home one day' - US travellers impacted by nationwide ground stop Passengers throughout the country were impacted over what the Federal Aviation Administration (FAA) describes as “safety and communication issues”. US flights are allowed to take off again after a ground stop halted flights nationwide for hours due to an air traffic control communications issue. Travellers throughout the country were impacted over what the Federal Aviation Administration (FAA) describes as “safety and communication issues”.,"Sat, 14 Nov 2026 01:47:36 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/videos/c893ryz7lw33o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-11-15 03:26:07
BBC News,Western Europe,en,Watch: Cruise ship overturns after collision with tanker in New Orleans,Watch: Cruise ship overturns after collision with tanker in New Orleans,"Watch: Cruise ship overturns after collision with tanker in New Orleans Around 130 people were rescued from a cruise ship after it was struck by a tanker while docked in New Orleans,  Louisiana on Friday. About 130 people were rescued from a cruise ship after it was struck by a tanker while docked in New Orleans, Louisiana on Friday. Footage shows the American Heritage taking on water following the collision, which left 32 people in hospital with non-critical injuries. ""We have zero people unaccounted for, which truly is a miracle on the Mississippi,"" New Orleans Mayor Helena Moreno told reporters during a press conference. The cause of the collision remains under investigation, with a possible steering fault suspected. Read more on this story:'Miracle on Mississippi' - All passengers rescued after New Orleans cruise ship collision","Sat, 14 Nov 2026 19:14:33 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/videos/ck4gn4y77m40o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-11-15 03:26:07
BBC News,Western Europe,en,Watch: Divers explore US ship sunk off Florida coast to create new reef,Watch: Divers explore US ship sunk off Florida coast to create new reef,"Watch: Divers explore US ship sunk off Florida coast to create new reef The ship was intentionally sunk off the coast of Florida in order to create the world's largest artificial ocean reef. The ship, which was once the fastest in the world and longer than the Titanic, was intentionally sunk off the coast of Florida, in order to create the world's largest artificial ocean reef. The SS United States spent nearly 30 years docked in Philadelphia before it was moved to Florida, where it will become a home for sea life and an underwater attraction for marine enthusiasts. Activists unsuccessfully fought for the historic liner - which crossed the Atlantic Ocean hundreds of times - to be preserved as a museum. Read more about the ship here.","Fri, 13 Nov 2026 15:09:40 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/videos/ckqx5ryx905qo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-11-15 03:26:07
BBC News,Western Europe,en,Will the Eurovision Song Contest succeed in Asia?,Will the Eurovision Song Contest succeed in Asia?,"Will the Eurovision Song Contest succeed in Asia? The world’s biggest and longest-running song contest is coming to Asia, with eleven countries competing in Bangkok, Thailand on Saturday. The world’s biggest and longest-running song contest is coming to Asia, with eleven countries competing in Bangkok, Thailand on Saturday. There are many passionate Eurovision fans in the region, but the Asia contest is not widely known. The BBC’s South East Asia correspondent Jonathan Head explains everything you need to know about the competition, and if it's likely to be a hit.","Fri, 13 Nov 2026 02:24:11 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/videos/cxvg23n59y1ro?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-11-15 03:26:07
BBC News,Western Europe,en,Military cemetery decorum: The tradition behind Hegseth's Veterans Day reprimand,Military cemetery decorum: The tradition behind Hegseth's Veterans Day reprimand,"Military cemetery decorum: The tradition behind Hegseth's Veterans Day reprimand BBC's Bernd Debusmann breaks down why Defence Secretary Hegseth's group was reprimanded at Arlington National Cemetery and the history behind its strict rules of conduct. Earlier this week, a sentinel reprimanded a group that included US Defence Secretary Pete Hegseth at Arlington National Cemetery. The incident followed a wreath-laying ceremony marking Veterans Day. The Pentagon have since announced that Hegseth has given the soldier, Specialist Thomas Iglesias, a Joint Service Commendation Medal ""for the execution of his duties at the Tomb of the Unknown Soldier"". BBC's Bernd Debusmann breaks down why Hegseth's group was reprimanded, the history behind the strict rules governing visitor behaviour and the importance of maintaining an atmosphere of silence at the Tomb of the Unknown Soldier. Read more on the incident.","Thu, 12 Nov 2026 23:42:52 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/videos/cv5y1309dg3go?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-11-15 03:26:07
BBC News,Western Europe,en,'We've got to run': Inside ruined Ukraine power plant under constant drone threat,'We've got to run': Inside ruined Ukraine power plant under constant drone threat,"'We've got to run': Inside ruined Ukraine power plant under constant drone threat Dan Johnson visits one of the country's largest power stations, barely left standing after a ballistic missile strike by Russia. As peace talks between Ukraine and Russia continue, Ukrainians are facing a rolling schedule of planned power cuts because of intensified Russian attacks on their energy grid. Ukraine has responded by striking Russian oil refineries. Officials are concerned that life will quickly become unsustainable for millions of Ukrainians if power supplies cannot be maintained for at least four hours per day. Some privately warn that's close already, as some families are living with outages of up to 20 hours in Kyiv. Ukraine correspondent Dan Johnson visits one of the country's power stations on the front lines of the war. Devastated by a direct ballistic missile strike, repair work is hampered by frequent air raids. Read the BBC's dispatch from Ukraine.","Thu, 12 Nov 2026 13:02:46 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/videos/cm93rp3gxn6qo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-11-15 03:26:07
BBC News,Western Europe,en,Judo champion shakes up French  presidential race with surprise candidacy,Judo champion shakes up French  presidential race with surprise candidacy,"Judo champion shakes up French  presidential race with surprise candidacy Teddy Riner is one of the most popular figures in modern-day France, and one in five voters say they could support him. Apart from an overwhelming urge to pin one's opponent to the floor of the house, there is no obvious connection between politics and the world of top-level judo. That has not stopped France's much-garlanded world heavyweight champion Teddy Riner from throwing his hat into the ring for next April's presidential race. The Guadeloupe-born 37-year-old may have no experience of public office, no party behind him, and as yet no manifesto. All so far is a sloganLa France qu'on aime(The France we love), a declaration on social media, and a campaign visit to a hospital in the Seine-Saint-Denis suburb of northern Paris. But it would be a foolhardy pundit who would totally write off his chances. Riner is, after all, one of the most popular figures in modern-day France, regularly topping the polls for embodying the nation's ""values"". He is a proven winner, with five Olympic golds and 11 world championships under his belt, as well as a successful career in business. More than 28 million people have watched his election broadcast on Instagram and X, shot with proper presidential decorum against a backdrop of books and a French flag. Some 22% told an Ifop poll within 24 hours they ""could"" vote for him. Actors and television personalities have become presidents in other countries. The ex-leader of Liberia – George Weah – was formerly a professional footballer. So why not a judoka in the Élysée Palace? ""Anyone who thinks that being out of the political in-crowd disqualifies Riner from running","Sun, 15 Nov 2026 00:06:51 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c6dj1e8wyykmo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-11-15 03:26:07
BBC News,Western Europe,en,My mother told me I would die if I sought treatment for Ebola - I am proof you can survive,My mother told me I would die if I sought treatment for Ebola - I am proof you can survive,"My mother told me I would die if I sought treatment for Ebola - I am proof you can survive Fear and misinformation surrounds the Ebola outbreak in DR Congo - Dr Kamwanga's mother was not immune. When Dr Raymond Kasereka Kamwanga told his family he suspected he had Ebola and was going to a treatment centre in the city of Butembo in eastern Democratic Republic of Congo, his family were horrified. They regarded the specialised unit as a ""death house"" as not many patients do recover - especially those admitted in the late stages of the illness. His 50-year-old mother was in tears, pleading with him not to go there. ""Why are you choosing to die at the Ebola treatment centre?"" Masika Ndungo had asked him. But as a doctor at Butembo's Katwa Hospital, he knew that if stayed at home, he was unlikely to survive. This was in July, two months after the World Health Organization (WHO) had declared the Ebola outbreak in DR Congo a public health emergency of international concern. Since then 5,400 people have died and 11,000 have been infected, making it the second-most deadly outbreak ever recorded. Butembo is in North Kivu province, the epicentre of the crisis, with the highest number of reported cases, according to the Congolese health ministry. Dr Kamwanga is one of 3,031 people who have survived - and is evangelical about sharing his story in an effort to help stop the spread of infections devastating his community. After staring death in the face and overcoming ostracisation, he has even started writing a book about his experience. ""As someone who has recovered, I am living proof that the disease exists, but also that it is possible to recover from it,"" he tells the BBC outsi","Sun, 15 Nov 2026 00:06:55 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cmvg2e2q28w1o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-11-15 03:26:07
BBC News,Western Europe,en,How telecoms upgrade work sparked US flight chaos,How telecoms upgrade work sparked US flight chaos,"How telecoms upgrade work sparked US flight chaos US officials say the disruption was caused by old equipment overloading and not a cyber-attack. An upgrade to a decades-old telecoms network in the US caused the longest nationwide grounding of flights since the 11 September 2001 attacks. Friday's stoppage occurred when part of a key system that allows air traffic controllers and pilots to communicate was switched to fibre from copper wiring so old it has been discontinued, said US Transportation Secretary Sean Duffy on Saturday. US planes were grounded for more than four hours, affecting thousands of flights and passengers. Earlier Homeland Security Secretary Markwayne Mullin said there was no indication of a cyber-attack and that everybody was safe. Here's what we know. On Friday afternoon, a government contractor called L3Harris was continuing work on upgrading an aviation telecoms network. As it swapped out copper lines for fibre at a remote location in the US, L3Harris installed a router - a piece of equipment which basically directs traffic across the network. According to Duffy, that router started flooding others in the air traffic control system, sending 1,000 connection attempts every four to five seconds. As a result, 17 out of 22 air route traffic control centres in the US experienced ""instability"" on voice and ground communications, said Bryan Bedford, administrator of the Federal Aviation Administration (FAA). It took the aviation authority 45 minutes to identify where the problem was because maintenance is logged manually on paper. This meant the FAA had to ""pore through"" the physical documents ""to figure","Sat, 14 Nov 2026 23:11:27 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cqx2wyvyr3zzo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-11-15 03:26:07
BBC News,Western Europe,en,Jojo the gorilla has spent his life alone. Can he finally find a companion?,Jojo the gorilla has spent his life alone. Can he finally find a companion?,"Jojo the gorilla has spent his life alone. Can he finally find a companion? Jojo was poached as an infant and grew up in captivity. His keepers want to find him a friend - but it's not straightforward. In 2015, a young male gorilla named Jojo took his first tentative steps onto grass. Until that moment, the 11-year-old ape had spent almost his entire life in a tiny cage at a private zoo in Gabon, after being taken from the wild as an infant. His family had been killed by the poachers who took him. More than a decade later, Jojo is settled into a sanctuary – but he still lives alone. He hasn't had a family since he was taken, or indeed any gorilla friends. Jojo's carers at the Lekedi Foundation in Gabon want to find a companion - possibly another rescued or zoo-bred gorilla – that Jojo could live and bond with. But finding him a friend - and bringing them into his life - is not straightforward. ""He's probably the most extreme case of isolation I've ever seen in a great ape,"" says Prof Gillian Forrester from the University of Sussex, who is studying Jojo as part of research into great ape psychology and how to help rescued animals recover from trauma. Jojo was less than a year old – an infant clinging to his mother – when he was captured and sold. Most gorillas spend the first 14 to 19 years of their life in an embedded family unit but he was kept completely alone and on public display at the private zoo that bought him. Maxe Anaclet Makaba, who is head keeper at Lekedi and sees Jojo nearly every day, says it is clear he ""craves human interaction"". ""He will even go to the trouble of gathering leaves for us to hold, so that we [trade them] for a treat – to interact with us,"" h","Sat, 14 Nov 2026 00:03:56 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cwm2mn272nqlo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-11-15 03:26:07
BBC News,Western Europe,en,"After a summer win, India's Gen Z protesters face a winter of uncertainty","After a summer win, India's Gen Z protesters face a winter of uncertainty","After a summer win, India's Gen Z protesters face a winter of uncertainty A new wave of youth dissent swept India this summer, fuelled by frustration and irreverent online satire. Cheers erupted at Delhi's Jantar Mantar protest site in July as news broke that Gen-Z protests had forced federal education minister Dharmendra Pradhan to resign. One protester turned to me, beaming: ""This is the youth's victory."" Just over three months later, the mood has shifted dramatically. Student protesters are now struggling even to assemble. Demonstrations by the Gen Z-led Cockroach Janta Party (CJP) last month over voting rights were met with sweeping restrictions andthousands of peaceful protesters were detained. The summer had seen a new wave of dissent in India, driven by deep frustration among the youth and fuelled by irreverent online satire. The movement spread across the country, and Prime Minister Narendra Modi, a leader with a strongman image, unaccustomed to open criticism or mockery, struggled to contain it. But the government's increasingly forceful security response suggests it has learned from its rare concession to protesters, invoking public order to curb dissent. The ruling Bharatiya Janata Party (BJP) national spokesperson Syed Zafar Islam rejected accusations of heavy-handedness and told the BBC: ""The right to protest and freedom of expression is mandated by the constitution, but it has to be done within the law. It cannot override the rights of others, at the cost of public security"". This is by no means the first time the Modi government has faced large-scale protests. But the breadth of them pose a challenge for the BJP, according to Yamini Aiya","Fri, 13 Nov 2026 23:11:51 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cq99e0z4vg1eo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-11-15 03:26:08
Deutsche Welle (EN),Western Europe,en,Nigeria's upcoming election marred by rising AI disinformation,Nigeria's upcoming election marred by rising AI disinformation,"Nigeria's upcoming election marred by rising AI disinformation As Nigeria inches closer to the 2027 polls, the use of artificial intelligence in the electoral process has prompted questions about integrity and institutional readiness. Can Nigerians trust their democratic processes? Nigeriahas just over two months to go until its general election, with incumbentBola Tinuburunning on a ticket focused on combatingraging insecurityand resolving the nation'seconomic crisis. He faces off against former Vice President Atiku Abubakar and senior opposition figure Peter Obi. The country's electoral body, the Independent National Electoral Commission (INEC), recently released its preliminary voter's register, which reportedly is riddled with images believed to have been generated usingartificial intelligence (AI). Among the telltale signs of AI usage were exaggerated facial features in images and inconsistencies on the electoral registers, such as duplicates of names and even identities of people who since the previous election had died. The development raises concerns about the body's overall preparedness ahead of the country's upcoming 2027 general election. Instead of making the upcoming vote more transparent by allowing access to its procedures, INEC has come under fire amid broader controversies about the expanding influence of AI in elections across the continent. To view this video please enable JavaScript, and consider upgrading to a web browser thatsupports HTML5 video Joash Amupitan, the chairman of the electoral commission, said the body is now investigating the development, focusing on discrepancies potentially created by AI use. To this end, he announced the establishment of a dedicated unit to monitor AI-related threats, detect deepfakes and strength",No Date,https://www.dw.com/en/nigeria-s-upcoming-election-marred-by-rising-ai-disinformation/a-79946897?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-rdf,2026-11-15 03:26:09
Deutsche Welle (EN),Western Europe,en,Fact check: 'Iranian' content farms reach millions,Fact check: 'Iranian' content farms reach millions,"Fact check: 'Iranian' content farms reach millions Several social media accounts claiming to represent the Iranian military show signs of being part of so-called content farms. DW Fact Check looks at how they operate and how users can spot them. As fears of regional escalation grew after US strikes on Iran, US President Donald Trump claimed theUS-Israel war with Iranwas ""effectively over"" on November 9 at aWhite House briefing. ""We're just keeping it the status quo."" Periods of heightened public interest, such as the conflict involving Iran, create fertile ground for so-calledcontent farms. Following Trump's statement, posts on the Iran war surged online again. And a growing network of accounts claiming links to Iran's military has accumulated millions of views. DW Fact Checkexamined a number of these accounts and found recurring signs of content farm behavior. Experts who reviewed the accounts DW provided describe them as part of a broader ecosystem of pro-Iran content aggregators that connect across multiple social media platforms. Content farmsare websites or social media accounts, or a network of either, producing large amounts of cheap content in a short time span. They may be driven by economic incentives, but they can also serve as vehicles for political influence campaigns. The internet watchdogNewsGuardhas found165 false claims about the Iran warthat collectively have been watched hundreds of millions of times. Only a few of them originated from Iranian state media. NewsGuard identified the majority as pushing a pro-Iran agenda, shifting toward AI-generated images, and increasingly claiming that accurate stories from authentic media are AI-generated and false. An analysis by the London-based Institute for St",No Date,https://www.dw.com/en/fact-check-iranian-content-farms-reach-millions/a-79934190?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-rdf,2026-11-15 03:26:10
Deutsche Welle (EN),Western Europe,en,"COP31: Protesters, UN urge swift climate deal","COP31: Protesters, UN urge swift climate deal","COP31: Protesters, UN urge swift climate deal The UN climate chief has warned that ""tactical delays"" at the COP31 climate talks in Turkey could prevent a deal to slow global warming. TheUnited Nations'top climate official urged negotiators at theCOP31climate summit inTurkeyto ""avoid tactical delays"" that would prevent a deal to stop global warming. Delegates from more than 200 nationsare gathered in the Mediterranean city of Antalya with the core goal of preventing the planet from warming by 2 degrees Celsius (3.6 degrees Fahrenheit). Downtown, thousands of civil society and environmental activists from around the world protested against the consequences ofclimate change. To view this video please enable JavaScript, and consider upgrading to a web browser thatsupports HTML5 video Simon Stiell, executive secretary of the United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change (UNFCCC), said getting an agreement is ""tough but vital work."" ""There ​is no collective success without compromise from every party,"" he added. ""As we head into the second week, ​I urge ‌parties to move swiftly to the hardest issues and to avoid tactical delays."" Meanwhile, tens of thousands of protesters marched through Antalya on Saturday for climate protection and human rights, according to organizers. Many were dismayed at the lack of progress and how the climate crisis is taking a back seat. ""The pursuit of profit at all costs continues to set the [global] agenda [and] govern the trajectory of our world, so there is a need to push back,"" environmental activist Zaki Mamdoo from South Africa told DW. Luisa Nebauer, the main organizer of Germany's Fridays for Future movement, lamented",No Date,https://www.dw.com/en/cop31-protesters-un-urge-swift-climate-deal/a-79948501?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-rdf,2026-11-15 03:26:10
Deutsche Welle (EN),Western Europe,en,Ukraine: Zelenskyy says security deal could go to referendum,Ukraine: Zelenskyy says security deal could go to referendum,"Ukraine: Zelenskyy says security deal could go to referendum The US has broached the idea of Kyiv giving up the Donbas region in exchange for NATO membership, the Ukrainian president said. Ukrainian PresidentVolodymyr Zelenskyysaid US peace envoys asked whether Kyiv would withdraw from its eastern region of ‌Donbas ⁠in exchange ⁠for joiningNATO, according to remarks to the media published on Saturday. The suggestion was made following the latest round ofpeace talksin Abu Dhabi to bringRussia'snearly 5-year war inUkraineto a close. The Ukrainian leader told reporters: ""The Americans raised a question: 'What if Ukraine joins NATO? Would ⁠Ukraine ​withdraw from Donbas?'"" Zelenskyy repeated Kyiv's position that it was not willing to cede territory to Russia. But he added that if the country reached a comprehensive deal, including security guarantees, a ‌strong ​and well-funded Ukrainian army and funds to rebuild, he would put the question ​to the public in a referendum. ""That ​is why ⁠I believe NATO is back on the ⁠agenda. ​But you have also heard [Russian President Vladimir]Putin'ssignals, and this is an extremely difficult conversation."" Zelenskyy said the prospect of NATO membership in exchange for withdrawing from Donbas has not been raised before. He also warned about Moscow's plan tochange the continent's security architecture, saying it could lead to more conflict. Putin has demanded that NATO pull back its troops from the Russian border, which European Union leaders have strongly criticized. ""This is dangerous because, in my view, it is precisely things like this that lead to wars or escalations,"" Zelenskyy added. ""Real security in Europe means the EU and N",No Date,https://www.dw.com/en/ukraine-zelenskyy-says-security-deal-could-go-to-referendum/a-79947535?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-rdf,2026-11-15 03:26:11
Deutsche Welle (EN),Western Europe,en,New facilities in Laos and Cambodia: Is China expanding its military presence in Southeast Asia?,New facilities in Laos and Cambodia: Is China expanding its military presence in Southeast Asia?,"New facilities in Laos and Cambodia: Is China expanding its military presence in Southeast Asia? China has been growing its economic relationships in Southeast Asia. Now new military facilities in Laos and Cambodia are causing smaller countries to worry China's military networks are also expanding. It's often said that maintaining good relations with your neighbors is essential. So in early November, China's Foreign Minister Wang Yi invited his counterparts from Laos, Cambodia, Vietnam, Thailand and Myanmar to Beijing. There the six countries agreed on closer economic and security cooperation. The visit is just one example of China'sgrowing influence on Southeast Asia. It's not just about trade or infrastructure either, it's also increasingly about military cooperation. For instance, at the end of September,ChinaandLaosopened a joint military support and training center north of the Laotian capital, Vientiane. Initially Laotian air force pilots are to be trained at the Ban Keun airfield, China has said. Earlier in 2025 China expanded its footprint atCambodia's Ream naval base. China uses the facility on the Gulf of Thailand for logistics and training. And while Japanese and Vietnamese ships can visit the port, they're docked at a southern quay and it's unclear if other navies can use the same facilities under the same conditions as China. ""It remains to be seen how this [Ban Keun] will change China's military access to Southeast Asia,"" says Marco Bünte, a professor of Asian politics at the University Erlangen Nuremberg in Germany. In recent years, China has significantly expanded its economic influence in the region. The China-Laos railway and other infrastructure projects connect Laos more closely with its northern neighbor. Laos is also heavily indebted to China. Bünt",No Date,https://www.dw.com/en/new-facilities-in-laos-and-cambodia-is-china-expanding-its-military-presence-in-southeast-asia/a-79944798?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-rdf,2026-11-15 03:26:11
Deutsche Welle (EN),Western Europe,en,Microsoft says all services working again,Microsoft says all services working again,"Microsoft says all services working again US tech giant Microsoft says it has fixed problems that led to a global outage of its services. The glitch severely disrupted online communications in businesses and governments for more than 24 hours. The US technology conglomerate Microsoft said on Saturday that a more than24-hour global outage affecting its Microsoft 365 applicationsused by businesses and governments across the world has now ended. ""We ​have completed our efforts to address ​the underlying issue of the ⁠incident ​and ​restored service to ​the remaining impacted ‌customers and regions,"" ​the ⁠company posted on X. Microsoft applications such as Teams, Outlook and Copilot were all hit by the outage, leading to communications problems in scores of countries. The company has said it was ""not aware"" of any outside interference that could have been behind the outage, blaming the problem on server issues. The disruption was one of the longest in Microsoft's history and the second this year. To view this video please enable JavaScript, and consider upgrading to a web browser thatsupports HTML5 video The outage, which began around 1725 UTC on Thursday according to web testing and network diagnostics company ​Ookla, affected government agencies and businesses across much of the world. It made it impossible to carry out activities such as sending emails and holding online meetings. Germany was no exception, with a spokesperson for its Federal Ministry for Digital Transformation and Government Modernization saying that while the outage did not have a major impact on federal government functions, it did significantly affect ​German businesses. Britain's state health service (NHS) was also severely affected, with perso",No Date,https://www.dw.com/en/microsoft-says-all-services-working-again/a-79946887?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-rdf,2026-11-15 03:26:12
Deutsche Welle (EN),Western Europe,en,Sudan: Misery worsens amid plummeting aid for displaced people,Sudan: Misery worsens amid plummeting aid for displaced people,"Sudan: Misery worsens amid plummeting aid for displaced people Sudan's humanitarian crisis is exacerbated by cuts in international aid. Almost 34 million people are in need as war and drone strikes are making it difficult to deliver aid to the population. The International Organization for Migration (IOM) and the United Nations High Commissioner for Refugees (UNHCR) are sounding the alarm: Serious funding gaps have depleted the supplies of emergency shelter and basic household goods intended forSudan. Without urgent provision of flexible funding, even more families will have to cope without adequate protection from extreme weather conditions, insecurity, violence and exploitation in the coming months. According to a recently publishedjoint statement, $2.9 billion (€2.62bn) is needed for the United Nation's Humanitarian Needs and Response Plan. This funding is intended to provide life-saving aid to 20.4 million people in Sudan. So far, however, only 47% of the required funds have been raised. A total of 33.7 million people in thewar- and famine-strickencountry are dependent on humanitarian aid. Some 15.4 million alone require emergency shelter and basic household items. However, due to a lack of funding, aid organizations have been able to assist only 10%. More thanthree and a half years after the war beganbetween the Sudanese Armed Forces (SAF) and paramilitary Rapid Support Forces (RSF), Sudan is still the site of the world's largest humanitarian and displacementcrisis. About 8.6 million people fled within the country to escape the escalating violence and more than 4.6 million fled to neighboring countries. Another 5.2 million have returned to their regions of origin despite the ongoing war. However,their towns and infrastruc",No Date,https://www.dw.com/en/sudan-misery-worsens-amid-plummeting-aid-for-displaced-people/a-79938864?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-rdf,2026-11-15 03:26:12
Deutsche Welle (EN),Western Europe,en,Turkey uses espionage accusations to pressure government critics,Turkey uses espionage accusations to pressure government critics,"Turkey uses espionage accusations to pressure government critics The arrest of a German foundation's employee in Istanbul this week has put the focus back on pressure tactics used by the Erdogan administration. The arrest is far from the first of its kind. When police officers appeared at her home inIstanbullast Wednesday evening, Yonca Verdioglu Sik was surprised — even more so when they took her in for questioning. Her lawyer says she was accused of political and military espionage. But, according to transcripts of the interrogation that were published later, Verdioglu Sik was never asked any questions about espionage. Instead the public prosecutor's office appeared to be more interested in the activities of her German employer, the Heinrich Böll Foundation. For 18 years, Verdioglu Sik, who was born and raised in Germany, has been the foundation's project coordinator. In her role, she organizes projects that focus on democracy and therights of women, minorities andLGBTQ+ people. During the interrogation, transfers of money from the foundation into her bank account were discussed. Verdioglu Sik found out that she was now also subject to a travel ban. Verdioglu Sik was eventually released, but her arrest caused outrage among critics of the Turkish government. She is married to investigative journalist and member of parliament, Ahmet Sik. He belongs to the left-wing Workers' Party of Turkey, or TIP, and is known as one of the harshest critics of the current government. Sik has recently been looking intoa stock market scandal, whichallegedly benefitedmembers of the ruling Justice and Development Party, orAKP, headed by Turkish President Recep Tayyip Erdogan, and associates. Opposition critics drew a clear conclusion: As a member ",No Date,https://www.dw.com/en/turkey-uses-espionage-accusations-to-pressure-government-critics/a-79943768?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-rdf,2026-11-15 03:26:13
Deutsche Welle (EN),Western Europe,en,Gen Z protests: Do Baby Boomers really have it better than young people?,Gen Z protests: Do Baby Boomers really have it better than young people?,"Gen Z protests: Do Baby Boomers really have it better than young people? Should young people in Germany be more worried about their future than Baby Boomers were in the 1980s and 1990s? Or should both generations take to the streets and protest together? DW checked the data. Young people acrossGermanyand Europe are taking to the streets, voicing fears of a bleak future. But is that really the case? Does Gen Z face greater challenges than Baby Boomers did in the 1990s? What do the two generations have in common, and where do their experiences differ? ""Of course, it's fair to ask, 'Were things just as tough for you back then?'"" youth researcher Simon Schnetzer tells DW. ""But young people see that things are difficult for them now, and say that's why they need support today."" Schnetzer, who wrote the annual Youth in Germany study, highlights one key question raised by therecent demonstrations across Europeas: ""Have we given young people the conditions they need to live and work here with confidence, and to keep this system funded?"" His straightforward answer is no. A survey of the statistics shows that both Gen Z and the Baby Boomers are affected by major societal changes, but in different ways. DW surveyed data on pensions, education, housing and climate change to find out more. The facts point to a clear trend: Fewer and fewer young people are having tosupport an ever-growing number of retirees. According to the Germany's public pension insurance system, there were 3.73 contributors for every retiree in 1957. By 2025, that figure had fallen to just 2.1. By 2045, this number is projected to drop to just 1.73 contributors per retiree. In absolute terms, this means 35.35 million contributors will be financingpensionsfor 16.82 million retirees. To cus",No Date,https://www.dw.com/en/gen-z-protests-do-baby-boomers-really-have-it-better-than-young-people/a-79918854?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-rdf,2026-11-15 03:26:13
Deutsche Welle (EN),Western Europe,en,"SpaceX launches Starship rocket, fails to recover booster","SpaceX launches Starship rocket, fails to recover booster","SpaceX launches Starship rocket, fails to recover booster SpaceX had planned to catch and later reuse both stages of its biggest rocket for the first time. But the booster exploded over the Gulf of Mexico, while the spacecraft continued into orbit. US company SpaceX has successfully launched the latest flight of its Starship spacecraft into orbit but did not recover the booster rocket as planned. The company, owned by tech billionaireElon Musk, is hoping to develop a fully reusable rocket system. It had planned to recover both the booster and the upper stage, which is the spacecraft itself. Instead of landing back at the launch site, the booster exploded above the Gulf of Mexico. The upper stage is currentlyin orbitand the plan is to recover it on Saturday when it returns to Earth. It is to be caught by mechanical arms upon landing back at the launch pad, marking the first time the spacecraft would be caught after returning to Earth. It could then potentially be reused in the future. In addition to testing the reusable rocket system, the mission also delivered 26 of SpaceX’ssatellitesfor itsStarlink network. SpaceX's Starship is the world's largest and most powerful rocket system. At 124 meters (407 feet) tall, it consists of a spacecraft mounted on a booster rocket. Both stages were designed from the outset to be fully reusable. At the launch, both the spacecraft and booster successfully lifted off from the SpaceX South Texas Starbase site. While the Friday launch was an uncrewed mission, Starship is designed to carry both crew and cargo. On its return path, the booster diverted from its flight path to the launch site and steered to a designated area over the Gulf of Mexico where it exploded over the sea surface. The r",No Date,https://www.dw.com/en/spacex-launches-starship-rocket-fails-to-recover-booster/a-79946091?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-rdf,2026-11-15 03:26:13
Deutsche Welle (EN),Western Europe,en,Tanker collides with cruise ship on Mississippi in New Orleans,Tanker collides with cruise ship on Mississippi in New Orleans,"Tanker collides with cruise ship on Mississippi in New Orleans A freighter collided with a cruise ship carrying around 130 people in New Orleans, causing the paddlewheel vessel to capsize. More than 30 people were hospitalized, but all passengers survived and were accounted for. More than 30 people were hospitalized but no deaths were reported after a freighter collided with a cruise ship on the Mississippi River in theUSon Friday. Authorities said at least 180 passengers and crew were rescued. New Orleans Mayor Helena Moreno described it as ""a miracle on the Mississippi,"" adding that none of those hospitalized had critical injuries. Footage showed the paddlewheel cruise boat, called The American Heritage, capsizing after the impact with the much larger cargo vessel and rescue teams rushing to get people in life jackets out of the water. The accident happened near the port of New Orleans when the freighter Atlantic Asphalt hit the cruise ship while it was docked. ""It was just chaos,"" local witness Devon Flanagan was quoted by news agency AP. ""You had all types of ships, like other barges and other companies down there trying to help out, which also was a little convoluted because you had people still in the water,"" he described the scene. The company operating The American Heritage , American Cruise Lines, said in a statement that ""guests and crew were evacuated, and we are deeply concerned for everyone involved."" According to AP, the cruise ship was built in 2015, is five stories tall and can hold up to 150 passengers. It was finishing an eight-day cruise to several Mississippi River destinations in Louisiana and Mississippi. When news of the collision broke, it was initially unclear whether there were any fatalities and how many people were injured",No Date,https://www.dw.com/en/tanker-collides-with-cruise-ship-on-mississippi-in-new-orleans/a-79945080?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-rdf,2026-11-15 03:26:14
Deutsche Welle (EN),Western Europe,en,The dangers of revising Europe's security architecture,The dangers of revising Europe's security architecture,"The dangers of revising Europe's security architecture Experts warn against revising Europe's security framework because they say it would mean granting Moscow its longstanding wish of renegotiating postwar security on the continent. European leaders have so far foundcompromises in response to many of US President Donald Trump's demands. But they were surprised to hearTrump say that he raised Europe's security frameworkwith Russian officials. ""It's going to be a big agreement and it's going to involve more than Ukraine,"" Trump said after meeting Russian presidential envoy Kirill Dmitriev at the White House last week. He said that deal might include all of Europe. But what he meant by ""all of Europe"" was not clear. US envoy Jared Kushner also wrote thatEurope and Russiahad struggled to establish a sustainable security framework for more than 30 years and that he was looking at ""broader"" European security architecturebefore he was to fly to Abu Dhabi. That sent off alarm bells in European capitals, with diplomats having pushed back on Washington's approach to secure a peace deal inUkraine. They feared that a peace settlement could restore Russia's sphere of influence and undermineNATO. ""We ​already have European security ​architecture in place. NATO and the European Union both have their ​respective roles. We ​must make sure Ukraine is integrated ‌into ⁠this architecture,"" said Kaja Kallas, the European Union's foreign policy chief. Russia made clear duringtalks in Abu Dhabiearlier this week that they were keen to discuss a broader European security framework before tackling the specifics of the war. ""Phrases about the European security architecture are certainly Russian talking points and not only talking ",No Date,https://www.dw.com/en/the-dangers-of-revising-europe-s-security-architecture/a-79943049?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-rdf,2026-11-15 03:26:14
Deutsche Welle (EN),Western Europe,en,Why does El Salvador's Bukele have troops in Haiti?,Why does El Salvador's Bukele have troops in Haiti?,"Why does El Salvador's Bukele have troops in Haiti? Haiti's Prime Minister congratulating El Salvador's President for a gang crackdown in Haiti has shone a light on the relationship between the two countries. What's going on, and where does the US stand? Haitihas been engulfed in crisis since 2018, with protests, apresidential assassinationandwidespread gang violencebringing the country to its knees. But earlier this week, Haiti's Prime Minister Alix Didier Fils-Aime hailed a major win in the battle against the violence, thanks to ""the commitment of the Salvadorean forces in the fight against gangs."" El Salvador's President, Nayib Bukelehad earlier said a joint task force between the countries had ""destroyed"" what he called ""the house of Lanmo San Jou and the 400 Mawozo gang's headquarters."" He also tweeted out an as-yet unverified video of helicopters firing on a sprawling property. A number of Salvadoran troops have been stationed in Haiti, nearly 2,000 kilometers from their homeland, since the beginning of 2025, mostly in an attempt to curb gang influence. Last month, Bukele dispatched more than 2,000 troops and special forces to Haiti, ostensibly to rescue four Salvadorans who were ambushed on October 18 and have not yet been found. To view this video please enable JavaScript, and consider upgrading to a web browser thatsupports HTML5 video Writing on social media platform X, Fils-Aime thanked the Salvadorean military for helping ""the fight against gangs."" The two nations are, he added, working alongside the UN-backed Gang Suppression Force (GSF), in an attempt to ""regain control of the territory and restore state authority."" The GSF has been operating in Haiti since 2016. But there are doubts to the legitimacy of El Salv",No Date,https://www.dw.com/en/why-does-el-salvador-s-bukele-have-troops-in-haiti/a-79940771?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-rdf,2026-11-15 03:26:14
France 24 (EN),Western Europe,en,SpaceX's Starship completes first-ever mid-air catch after return from orbit,SpaceX's Starship completes first-ever mid-air catch after return from orbit,"SpaceX's Starship completes first-ever mid-air catch after return from orbit SpaceX's Starship spacecraft made a successful return to Earth on Saturday, completing its first mid-air catch by giant robot arms in Texas, after an aborted recovery attempt. SpaceX's Starship spacecraft made a successful return to Earth on Saturday, completing its first mid-air catch by giant robot arms in Texas, after an aborted recovery attempt. Issued on:14/11/2026 - 21:51Modified:14/11/2026 - 22:45 SpaceX’s Starship made a triumphant return to Earth on Saturday, caught in midair by giant mechanical arms where its journey began 22 hours earlier. It was the grand finale that Elon Musk and his team hoped for — the first-ever capture of a Starship flying back from space. But with the first-stage booster exploding shortly after Friday’s liftoff from Texas, the company fell short of its desired double catch. Wreckage remained in the Gulf of Mexico, cordoned off by buoys, as the stainless steel, bullet-shaped spacecraft lowered itself upright at the launch pad. The dramatic, sci-fi-like capture helped blot out the booster’s messy miss. “Can you believe it?” shouted SpaceX commentator Dan Huot. SpaceX officials said Saturday’s catch demonstrates the full reusability of Starship, intended for moon and Mars travel. Although Friday’s booster recovery failed, the company had already made three previous booster catches, validating the technique. NASA welcomed Saturday’s news. The space agency is paying billions of dollars for Starship to land Artemis astronauts on the moon later this decade and help build a permanent lunar base. Musk’s ambitions are even grander: He wants to launch a fleet of Starships to build a city on Mars. The world’slargest and most ","Sat, 14 Nov 2026 20:51:24 GMT",https://www.france24.com/en/spacex-starship-set-for-return-to-earth-after-failed-booster-catch,2026-11-15 03:26:15
France 24 (EN),Western Europe,en,Netanyahu renews call for Israeli unity government as Eisenkot dismisses 'tricks',Netanyahu renews call for Israeli unity government as Eisenkot dismisses 'tricks',"Netanyahu renews call for Israeli unity government as Eisenkot dismisses 'tricks' Israeli caretaker Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu on Saturday renewed his call for a national unity government with Gadi Eisenkot, who dismissed the appeal as a political ploy. This comes as the incoming premier is in the midst of negotiations to form a new administration. Israeli caretaker Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu on Saturday renewed his call for a national unity government with Gadi Eisenkot, who dismissed the appeal as a political ploy. This comes as the incoming premier is in the midst of negotiations to form a new administration. Issued on:14/11/2026 - 21:35Modified:14/11/2026 - 22:16 Israel's caretaker prime ministerBenjamin Netanyahuon Saturday reiterated his calls for a national unitygovernmentwith his opponentGadi Eisenkot, who in turn dismissed the appeal as a ruse. The latest exchange comes as Eisenkotis in the midst of negotiations to form a government, after he was tapped for the post of premier on Sunday. Read moreIsrael's new parliament to be sworn in as Eisenkot prepares government A meeting with Eisenkot on Thursday ""only strengthened the conviction that it is essential to form a national unity government"" to guarantee Israel's security, Netanyahu said in a video statement, adding that such a government could ""eliminate the Iranian threat once and for all"". He said this government would be made up of parties that recognise Israel ""as a state for the Jewish people"". It would address security challenges, he added, most notablyIranwhich he claimed would ""not hesitate to usenuclear weaponsagainst us"". The outgoing prime minister warned against Eisenkot's plan to form a minority government, presented as a ""national emergency government"". Such a plan would see the 48 lawmakers in Eisenkot's bloc vote in favour of his governm","Sat, 14 Nov 2026 20:35:22 GMT",https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261114-netanyahu-renews-call-for-israeli-government-unity-as-eisenkot-dismisses-tricks,2026-11-15 03:26:15
France 24 (EN),Western Europe,en,Macron says limiting Ukraine aid would pave way to 'another war',Macron says limiting Ukraine aid would pave way to 'another war',"Macron says limiting Ukraine aid would pave way to 'another war' French President Emmanuel Macron on Saturday warned that limiting aid to Ukraine would force Kyiv into an ""unjust peace"" and risk another war. His statement comes as the United States pushes ahead with efforts to negotiate between Ukraine and Moscow. French President Emmanuel Macron on Saturday warned that limiting aid to Ukraine would force Kyiv into an ""unjust peace"" and risk another war. His statement comes as the United States pushes ahead with efforts to negotiate between Ukraine and Moscow. Issued on:14/11/2026 - 21:15 French PresidentEmmanuel Macronsaid Saturday that countries seeking to limit aid toUkrainewant to impose an ""unjust peace"" and warned that it was a recipe for another war. The French leader's statementposted on social mediacomes as the United States mediates talks between Ukraine andRussiain a bid to end the war that started withMoscow's 2022 full-scale invasion. ""Those who want to ration aid to Ukraine wish underneath it all to force an unjust peace on it"" and, by extension, all ofEurope, Macron said. ""That is the spirit of defeat. That is a recipe for another war. That cannot be France's position."" Ukrainian PresidentVolodymyr Zelenskysaid in comments released Saturday that Washington hassuggested Kyiv cede land to Moscowin exchange for Ukraine joining NATO. Read moreUS proposed Ukraine cedes Donbas in exchange for joining NATO, Zelensky says US envoysJared KushnerandSteve Witkoffmet both Russian PresidentVladimir Putinand Zelensky separately on Thursday, after two days of talks inAbu Dhabiwhich ended with no breakthrough. Negotiations did, however, lead to agreements that European countries will join future talks on ending the war. On Friday, in Dublin, Europe chose five countries –France,Germany,I","Sat, 14 Nov 2026 20:15:43 GMT",https://www.france24.com/en/europe/20261114-macron-says-limiting-ukraine-aid-would-pave-way-to-another-war,2026-11-15 03:26:15
France 24 (EN),Western Europe,en,"'No justice, no peace': Fifth round of protests sweep across France","'No justice, no peace': Fifth round of protests sweep across France","'No justice, no peace': Fifth round of protests sweep across France Between 106,000 and 200,000 people took to the streets across France on Saturday for the fifth round of protests, according to figures from the interior ministry and student unions respectively, as turnout fell and some incidents were reported. Between 106,000 and 200,000 people took to the streets across France on Saturday for the fifth round of protests, according to figures from the interior ministry and student unions respectively, as turnout fell and some incidents were reported. Issued on:14/11/2026 - 19:23Modified:14/11/2026 - 22:59 Tens of thousands of protesters paraded in the streets ofFranceon Saturday, some clambering up statues with French flags as they vented on issues ranging from the rising cost of living to a proposal to give police greater legal protection. TheEuropean Union's second-largest economy has been gripped by high-school blockades and mass demonstrations for more than a month, with the far right leading in the polls to winpresidential electionsnext year to replaceEmmanuel Macron. Around 106,000 people took to the streets nationwide, including 24,000 in Paris, the interior ministry said, in the lowest turnout in recent weeks. Student unions put the number of demonstrators at 200,000, including almost 100,000 in the capital. Arrest figureswere also noticeably lower than at previous mass protests, with the ministry saying 219 people had been detained across the nation. Paris prosecutors said 45 were arrested in the capital. ""No justice, no peace,"" a protester shouted into a megaphone in Paris as people including students, unions and Yellow Vest activists demonstrated for the fifth Saturday in a row. Others called for the president to resign, or cried ""Everybody hates the police"", as a group ","Sat, 14 Nov 2026 18:23:25 GMT",https://www.france24.com/en/france/20261114-no-justice-no-peace-fifth-round-of-protests-sweep-across-france,2026-11-15 03:26:15
France 24 (EN),Western Europe,en,Elon Musk's SpaceX aborts booster recovery due to technical issues,Elon Musk's SpaceX aborts booster recovery due to technical issues,"Elon Musk's SpaceX aborts booster recovery due to technical issues Elon Musk's SpaceX is in the midst of a 15th test-flight of its Starship rocket. The company was hoping to pull off the first-ever ""double catch"" - with both the booster and the ship itself coming safely back down to earth. Instead, an unspecified problem forced the company to abort recovery of the booster, which was diverted from its return path and crashed down into the Gulf of Mexico. Analysis by journalist Frédéric Castel, based in Bosa Chica, Texas, USA. To display this content from YouTube, you must enable advertisement tracking and audience measurement. One of your browser extensions seems to be blocking the video player from loading. To watch this content, you may need to disable it on this site. Issued on:14/11/2026 - 18:33 Elon Musk's SpaceX is in the midst of a 15th test-flight of its Starship rocket. The company was hoping to pull off the first-ever ""double catch"" - with both the booster and the ship itself coming safely back down to earth. Instead, an unspecified problem forced the company to abort recovery of the booster, which was diverted from its return path and crashed down into the Gulf of Mexico. Analysis by journalist Frédéric Castel, based in Bosa Chica, Texas, USA. Keywords for this article The content you requested does not exist or is not available anymore.","Sat, 14 Nov 2026 17:33:37 GMT",https://www.france24.com/en/elon-musk-s-spacex-aborts-booster-recovery-due-to-technical-issues,2026-11-15 03:26:15
France 24 (EN),Western Europe,en,Youth Olympic Games organisers hail Senegal's capability to host major events,Youth Olympic Games organisers hail Senegal's capability to host major events,"Youth Olympic Games organisers hail Senegal's capability to host major events Dakar 2026 is over. Two weeks of competition, thousands of young athletes and a historic first for Africa. The Youth Olympic Games brought the world to Senegal for the first Olympic sporting event ever held on the continent. From record-breaking performances to historic medals for the host nation, Dakar 2026 has been a celebration of sport, youth and cultural exchange. Our correspondent Aminatou Diallo reports from Dakar. To display this content from YouTube, you must enable advertisement tracking and audience measurement. One of your browser extensions seems to be blocking the video player from loading. To watch this content, you may need to disable it on this site. Issued on:14/11/2026 - 18:25 Dakar 2026 is over. Two weeks of competition, thousands of young athletes and a historic first for Africa. The Youth Olympic Games brought the world to Senegal for the first Olympic sporting event ever held on the continent. From record-breaking performances to historic medals for the host nation, Dakar 2026 has been a celebration of sport, youth and cultural exchange. Our correspondent Aminatou Diallo reports from Dakar. Keywords for this article The content you requested does not exist or is not available anymore.","Sat, 14 Nov 2026 17:25:51 GMT",https://www.france24.com/en/youth-olympic-games-organisers-hail-senegal-s-capability-to-host-major-events,2026-11-15 03:26:15
