France 24 (EN),Western Europe,en,Wales claim historic victory against All Blacks for first time since 1953,Wales claim historic victory against All Blacks for first time since 1953,"Wales claim historic victory against All Blacks for first time since 1953 Wales claimed a historic 35-28 victory over New Zealand in Cardiff on Saturday, securing their first win over the All Blacks since 1953. Wales claimed a historic 35-28 victory over New Zealand in Cardiff on Saturday, securing their first win over the All Blacks since 1953. Issued on:14/11/2026 - 17:27Modified:14/11/2026 - 18:22 WalesedgedNew Zealand35-28 in the Nations Championship in Cardiff on Saturday to claim their first victory over the All Blacks since 1953. Scrum-half Tomos Williams scored one of five Welsh tries in a superb individual performance as the home side ended a 34-match losing run against the three-timeRugby World Cupwinners. Wales's heroic defence held out the visitors in the final 25 minutes after the All Blacks had made it a seven-point game thanks to Peter Lakai's try. The victory is even more remarkable with Welshrugbyin dire straights after a poor run of results since the 2023 World Cup and a long line of off-field issues overshadowing the sport in the country. Coach Steve Tandy made five changes from last weekend's tight win overJapanincluding starting Adam Beard at lock alongside Dafydd Jenkins to improve success at lineout. New Zealandcoach Davie Rennie handed full-back Beauden Barrett the captaincy for the first time on his 149th Test appearance in eight alterations from last Saturday's win overScotland. After just seven minutes, the hosts raced to a surprise 14-0 lead thanks to Williams and hooker Dewi Lake with the scrum-half providing the assist for the front-rower. By the half-hour mark, Wales had a 21-7 lead with prop Archie Griffin diving over as New Zealand scrum-half Cortez ","Sat, 14 Nov 2026 16:27:21 GMT",https://www.france24.com/en/sport/20261114-wales-claim-historic-victory-against-all-blacks-for-first-time-since-1953,2026-11-15 03:26:16
France 24 (EN),Western Europe,en,'Poisoned chalice': French presidential hopeful Riner rebuffs Macron's backing,'Poisoned chalice': French presidential hopeful Riner rebuffs Macron's backing,"'Poisoned chalice': French presidential hopeful Riner rebuffs Macron's backing French judo champion Teddy Riner on Saturday publicly distanced himself from President Emmanuel Macron, calling his endorsement a ""poisoned chalice"". The presidential hopeful seeks to position himself as an alternative to France's political extremes. French judo champion Teddy Riner on Saturday publicly distanced himself from President Emmanuel Macron, calling his endorsement a ""poisoned chalice"". The presidential hopeful seeks to position himself as an alternative to France's political extremes. Issued on:14/11/2026 - 15:57Modified:14/11/2026 - 16:59 France's judo champion turned presidential candidateTeddy Rineron Saturday sought to distance himself from PresidentEmmanuel Macron, calling the head of state's rare endorsement a ""poisoned chalice"". The centrist president said on Monday thatRiner joining the 2027 election racecould ""only benefit the country"" after the popular judoka announced he was running. Read moreJudo star Teddy Riner announces bid for French presidency ""I'm not the president's envoy,"" said the 37-year-old, who has won five Olympic gold medals. ""If I had to describe the president's congratulations, it would be more like a poisoned chalice,"" he added as he visited a trade fair of products made inFrance. Riner said in his announcement on Monday he feared France could soon become deeply divided. He appeared to cast himself as a candidate for voters unwilling to choose between two political extremes — thefar rightand the hard left. The sportsman from the French overseas territory ofGuadeloupehas said he is neither from the left nor the right, but some have accused him of seeking to follow in Macron's footsteps. Macron's popularity is the lowest it has ever been since he first became president in 2017, accor","Sat, 14 Nov 2026 14:57:22 GMT",https://www.france24.com/en/france/20261114-judo-star-and-presidential-hopeful-riner-calls-macron,2026-11-15 03:26:16
France 24 (EN),Western Europe,en,"DR Congo, Rwanda clash over Francophonie leadership as Macron seeks compromise","DR Congo, Rwanda clash over Francophonie leadership as Macron seeks compromise","DR Congo, Rwanda clash over Francophonie leadership as Macron seeks compromise DR Congo and Rwanda clashed Saturday over the leadership of the Francophonie, as tensions between the neighbouring countries spilled into the race to head the 53-member organisation. French President Emmanuel Macron urged Rwanda to withdraw its candidate in favour of a compromise choice ahead of Monday's vote. DR Congo and Rwanda clashed Saturday over the leadership of the Francophonie, as tensions between the neighbouring countries spilled into the race to head the 53-member organisation. French President Emmanuel Macron urged Rwanda to withdraw its candidate in favour of a compromise choice ahead of Monday's vote. Issued on:14/11/2026 - 15:54 TheDemocratic Republic of CongoandRwanda, alreadymired in cross-border conflict, clashed on Saturday over who should next head a key group of French-speaking states and governments. The next secretary-general of the 53-member Organisation Internationale de la Francophonie (OIF), which begins a summit inCambodiaon Sunday, has become a major bone of contention. French PresidentEmmanuel Macron, who arrived Saturday in the capital Phnom Penh, has requested that Rwanda withdraw its candidate for the job, current leaderLouise Mushikiwabo, in favour of a compromise choice. Mushikiwabo has been standing for another term against three other candidates, including DR Congo's Juliana Lumumba, the daughter of national heroPatrice Lumumba. Following a story in Jeune Afrique magazine reporting Macron's move, Rwandan Foreign Minister Olivier Nduhungirehe said his country had ""rejected"" the request. DR Congo, the world's second-largest French-speaking country, has threatened to quit the organisation in the event that Mushikiwabo is re-elected on Monday, according to several sources. Kinshasa accuses Rwanda of supporting the M23 armed group's activities insid","Sat, 14 Nov 2026 14:54:43 GMT",https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20261114-dr-congo-rwanda-clash-over-francophonie-leadership-as-macron-seeks-compromise,2026-11-15 03:26:16
France 24 (EN),Western Europe,en,'I never imagined my father was a Nazi': Born in a German-run 'baby factory','I never imagined my father was a Nazi': Born in a German-run 'baby factory',"'I never imagined my father was a Nazi': Born in a German-run 'baby factory' Two trainloads of children, some of them born in Nazi-run ""Lebensborn"" maternity homes, were brought from Germany to Commercy in eastern France in 1946. One of those on board was Édith Pagot. Two trainloads of children, some of them born in Nazi-run ""Lebensborn"" maternity homes, were brought from Germany to Commercy in eastern France in 1946. One of those on board was Édith Pagot. Issued on:14/11/2026 - 14:25Modified:14/11/2026 - 16:38 To display this content from YouTube, you must enable advertisement tracking and audience measurement. One of your browser extensions seems to be blocking the video player from loading. To watch this content, you may need to disable it on this site. Adopted as a child, Édith Pagot had no idea she had been born in a Nazi-runLebensborn (“fount of life”) maternity homeuntil earlier this year, when her daughter began looking into their past. “This photo is of a baby about 18 months old. It has my first name and surname on it,” says Édith, holding a black-and-white photo between her fingertips. Until a few months ago, Édith had never seen a picture of herself as a baby. “I look nice. I look very well-behaved,” she says, a little wistfully. However, behind the image of the chubby-cheeked infant lies a dark story. “It was taken at the Indersdorf orphanage inGermanyin 1945. They photographed us holding a sign with our first names and surnames on it, to try to find our families,"" Édith says. ""It’s a bit difficult to see that sign,” she adds. At the time, the baby girl in the picture was still called Édith de Vicar. It was the name under which she was born on April 11, 1944, in a Lebensborn home inFrance's Oise department, before a journey th","Sat, 14 Nov 2026 13:25:35 GMT",https://www.france24.com/en/france/20261114-i-never-imagined-my-father-was-a-nazi-born-in-a-german-run-baby-factory,2026-11-15 03:26:16
