Al Jazeera (EN),Middle East (English),en,"More than 100,000 rally across France in new Yellow Vest protests","More than 100,000 rally across France in new Yellow Vest protests","More than 100,000 rally across France in new Yellow Vest protests Protesters chant against declining affordability, the Macron government, police violence, and poor school conditions. Save Share More than 100,000 people have taken to the streets in France in another round of cost-of-living rallies organised by the “Yellow Vests” and various youth movements. Saturday’s protests saw a decline in turnout compared with previous weekends, and stayed largely calm, authorities said. The Ministry of the Interior reported that 106,000 people took part in protests nationwide, including 24,000 in Paris – down from 146,000 people nationwide and 46,000 in Paris last week. Student unions place Saturday’s protest numbers higher, at 200,000 people, including almost 100,000 in the capital. Arrest figures on Saturday were also lower than at previous mass protests. The ministry said 219 people were detained across the country, while Paris prosecutors recorded 45 arrests in the capital. France has been gripped by high-school blockades, mass demonstrations, and strikes for more than a month, with protesters rallying against falling educational standards, cost-of-living increases, low wages, and police violence. Several demonstrators also carried signs critical of President Emmanuel Macron. Some rallies in recent weeks have seen violence at the fringes, and the first major protest on October 17 saw looting in several cities. Since then, the police presence at rallies and controls on transport and permits to protest have been expanded. Marseille, France’s second-largest city, banned protests over the weekend, but the prefecture announced that some 3,500 people still took to the ","Sat, 14 Nov 2026 19:46:24 +0000",https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/11/14/more-than-100000-rally-across-france-in-new-yellow-vest-protests?traffic_source=rss,2026-11-15 03:26:20
Al Jazeera (EN),Middle East (English),en,Tens of thousands march in latest Portugal housing protests,Tens of thousands march in latest Portugal housing protests,"Tens of thousands march in latest Portugal housing protests These were the biggest protests against rising housing costs in Portugal since the social movement began weeks ago. Save Share Tens of thousands of people have taken to the streets of more than 30 towns and cities in Portugal to protest against surging rental prices, demanding urgent government action to address the country’s worsening housing crisis. Organised by the Casa Para Viver (A Home to Live In) platform, demonstrations on Saturday took place under the slogan “They will not steal our right to a home,” with protests in Lisbon, Porto, Faro, Portimao, Coimbra, Braga and other locations. It was the biggest national demonstrationsince the social movement began weeks ago. Participants, ranging from teenagers to retired people, walked behind large banners, with the Lisbon march punctuated by drums, music, and chanting. Protesters called for caps to be placed on rent increases, greater legal protection for tenants against eviction, and more affordable housing. They also demanded the reversal of proposed changes to rental legislation. One woman on a protest march in Lisbon told Al Jazeera that she was a business owner, “and I can’t afford a house.” “I can’t say much more than this. I’m forty-years-old, I can’t go to a bank and just buy a house with my money,” she added. Another woman told Al Jazeera that she had joined the march to protest “for a better life, for all of us, not just the young people, all of us, because we deserve to have a home.” One man told Al Jazeera “we have been suffering this for the last three or four years, and I have many friends and family in this situation, they ","Sat, 14 Nov 2026 19:27:55 +0000",https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/11/14/tens-of-thousands-march-in-latest-portugal-housing-protests?traffic_source=rss,2026-11-15 03:26:21
Al Jazeera (EN),Middle East (English),en,Pope Leo urges end to violence in Peru during visit to remote Andes town,Pope Leo urges end to violence in Peru during visit to remote Andes town,"Pope Leo urges end to violence in Peru during visit to remote Andes town In Santa Cruz de Succhabamba, the pope told people not to respond to injustice with brutality. Save Share Pope Leo XIV has called for an end to the violence gripping Peru, urging people not to respond to injustice with brutality, as he visitedcommunities he once served as bishop. “We feel threatened by the uncertainty of widespread violence and by the unspeakable suffering that continues to afflict so many of our brothers and sisters, he said, speaking at his former cathedral in Chiclayo. Peru’s homicide rate has nearly doubled over the past five years, from 5.8 to 10.7 per 100,000 people. Reported extortion cases rose from 2,421 in 2019 to 26,734 in 2025, according to official figures cited by The Associated Press. Security was a top concern for Peruvians in the last election. Conservative President Keiko Fujimoritook office in Julyafter promising to tackle crime. She has since declared states of emergency, including in Lima in August, then in September in prisons holding inmates linked to criminal organisations. She has also pursued closer ties with Washington,bringing Peru into the ‘Shield of the Americas,’a US-led regional coalition formed to carry out joint military operations against drug cartels. Later on Saturday, in the Andean town of Santa Cruz de Succhabamba with a population of about 6,000, about 20,000 people came to an outdoor Mass, where Leo also addressed violence in society. “Selfishness, injustice and evil are not overcome by brutality, but by prayer, patience and perseverance in denouncing what is wrong, by making our pleas with determination and wor","Sat, 14 Nov 2026 19:20:11 +0000",https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/11/14/pope-leo-urges-end-to-violence-in-peru-during-visit-to-remote-andes-town?traffic_source=rss,2026-11-15 03:26:21
