Al Jazeera (EN),Middle East (English),en,US blames system upgrade for longest nationwide FAA ground stop since 9/11,US blames system upgrade for longest nationwide FAA ground stop since 9/11,"US blames system upgrade for longest nationwide FAA ground stop since 9/11 Sean Duffy, the country&#039;s transportation secretary, said his agency is &#039;going to hold people accountable&#039;. Save Share The top United States transportation official has said work being performed on an antiquated telecommunications system could be to blame for a rare, hours-long US Federal Aviation Administration (FAA)ground stopthat halted all air traffic inside the country. At a news conference in Washington, DC, on Saturday, US Transportation Secretary Sean Duffy spoke about routine maintenance that was performed on Friday to upgrade a router from copper to fibre at a “very remote location”. Duffy said “contractor error or a faulty piece of equipment” caused the router in question to start flooding other ones in the country’s air traffic control system. “We had 1,000 connection attempts every four to five seconds,” he told reporters at the Department of Transportation. When those connection issues caused disruption in radar feeds and communications between air traffic controllers and aeroplanes, Duffy said a ground stop was ordered. “When we see that, your safety is paramount. We shut down the airspace,” he said. The Transportation Department identified the contractor of Friday’s maintenance as L3Harris, a US-based aerospace and defence company that has been tasked with modernising the communications system for the FAA. “What we’re working to determine was whether we had incompetence, meaning was it … installator error from L3Harris? Or did they install a faulty router?” Duffy said. “We haven’t determined what the fault was.” Duffy added that L3Harris, which he said had performed","Sat, 14 Nov 2026 18:02:53 +0000",https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/11/14/us-blames-system-upgrade-for-longest-nationwide-faa-ground-stop-since-9?traffic_source=rss,2026-11-15 03:26:21
