The Hindu,South Asia,en,"U.S. air travel should recover from ground stop by end of November 15, FAA says","U.S. air travel should recover from ground stop by end of November 15, FAA says","U.S. air travel should recover from ground stop by end of November 15, FAA says New technology being added to the nation's air traffic control system overloaded antiquated equipment and caused “intermittent” communications and radar gaps, leading to the flight shutdown of more than four hours, Trump administration officials said You are logged in Loading... You don’t have any Active Subscription. Subscribed with another email? Logout and Login with that one. Your active subscription(s) Account subscription benefits alongside Premium Stories, Editorials, Opinions and more. Unlock these with Subscription Products you've access to Additional Subscription Benefits Account Settings Need help with your subscription? Updated- November 15, 2026 08:26 am IST - Washington Travelers wait in a Transportation Security Administration (TSA) line after the U.S. Federal Aviation Administration lifted a national ground stop, at Ronald Reagan Washington National Airport in Arlington, Virginia, U.S., on November 14, 2026. | Photo Credit: Reuters U.S. air travel should be fully recovered from the longest nationwide ground stop in 25 years by the end of Sunday, FAA Administrator Bryan Bedford said on Saturday (November 14, 2026). Mr. Bedford said the flights “will still have delays and cancellations” on Saturday (November 14) but promised that the “worst is behind us.” His comments came as airlines across the U.S. worked to recover from a ground stoppage that lasted four hours, stranding hundreds of thousands of passengers. New technology being added to the nation's air traffic control system overloaded antiquated equipment and caused “intermittent” communications and radar gaps, leading to the flight shutdown of more than four hours, Trump administration officials said. Planes were taking flight and landing on Saturday (","Sun, 15 Nov 2026 08:26:54 +0530",https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/us-air-travel-should-recover-from-ground-stop-by-end-of-november-15-faa-says/article71703985.ece,2026-11-15 03:30:50
The Hindu,South Asia,en,Judge temporarily blocks Trump from moving ahead on Washington public golf course redevelopment,Judge temporarily blocks Trump from moving ahead on Washington public golf course redevelopment,"Judge temporarily blocks Trump from moving ahead on Washington public golf course redevelopment U.S. District Judge Ana Reyes issued the temporary restraining order before the East Potomac Golf Links was due to close on November 15 for 15 months You are logged in Loading... You don’t have any Active Subscription. Subscribed with another email? Logout and Login with that one. Your active subscription(s) Account subscription benefits alongside Premium Stories, Editorials, Opinions and more. Unlock these with Subscription Products you've access to Additional Subscription Benefits Account Settings Need help with your subscription? Published- November 15, 2026 08:24 am IST - Washington U.S. President Donald Trump. File | Photo Credit: Reuters A federal judge on Saturday (November 14, 2026) ordered a temporary stop to any work at a historic Washington public golf course not far from the White House, blocking for now President Donald Trump's push to redevelop the site along the Potomac River into a championship-level venue. U.S. District Judge Ana Reyes issued the temporary restraining order before the East Potomac Golf Links was due to close on Sunday (November 15) for 15 months. Mr. Trump had announced work would begin that day despite the legal challenge initiated against the project in February by the DC Preservation League and two local golfers. The plaintiffs argue that Mr. Trump's plan would change the character of the accessible golf course and make it unaffordable, something the Republican president denies. They had sought the stop-work order, saying that closing the course and launching work on the project would ""irreparably injure plaintiffs and the public interest."" The park's redevelopment is one of multiple pr","Sun, 15 Nov 2026 08:24:53 +0530",https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/judge-temporarily-blocks-trump-from-moving-ahead-on-washington-public-golf-course-redevelopment/article71704032.ece,2026-11-15 03:30:50
The Hindu,South Asia,en,M. John Harrison | From rebellion to despair,M. John Harrison | From rebellion to despair,"M. John Harrison | From rebellion to despair The British author who won this year’s Booker Prize has long championed resistance to the status quo, but his latest novel reflects a deepening pessimism about humanity and its future You are logged in Loading... You don’t have any Active Subscription. Subscribed with another email? Logout and Login with that one. Your active subscription(s) Account subscription benefits alongside Premium Stories, Editorials, Opinions and more. Unlock these with Subscription Products you've access to Additional Subscription Benefits Account Settings Need help with your subscription? Updated- November 15, 2026 08:18 am IST Illustration: Sreejith R. Kumar In 2019, British writer M. John Harrison said he supportedExtinction Rebellion, a global environmental movement, and people taking action to change the world. But last week, after winning the Booker Prize for his “destabilising and satirical dystopian novel”,The End of Everything,he said he had lost hope for humanity — even as people continued to fight to change the world. Did something change in seven years? Harrison was born in 1945 and grew up in the shadow of the Second World War. He has said he was impressed byThe Waste Landby T.S. Eliot, especially the way its narrative could pull fragments together into a coherent whole without also pretending it depicted a complete reality. Harrison later moved to London from Warwickshire determined to become a professional writer, despite financial difficulties. Published- November 15, 2026 01:45 am IST The Hindu Profiles/World literature/authors and poets/arts, culture and entertainment/award-winning books/books and literature/English literature Terms & conditions|Institutional Su","Sun, 15 Nov 2026 08:18:01 +0530",https://www.thehindu.com/books/books-authors/m-john-harrison-from-rebellion-to-despair/article71702830.ece,2026-11-15 03:30:51
