n and reprimand” in recent months, indicating that there was more ","Sat, 14 Nov 2026 17:12:22 +0530",https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/us-media-names-indian-official-who-allegedly-ordered-plot-on-pannun/article68121919.ece,2026-11-15 03:30:56
The Hindu,South Asia,en,What are the legal compulsions and political hurdles in Sheikh Hasina’s return to Bangladesh? | Explained,What are the legal compulsions and political hurdles in Sheikh Hasina’s return to Bangladesh? | Explained,"What are the legal compulsions and political hurdles in Sheikh Hasina’s return to Bangladesh? | Explained Sheikh Hasina wants to return to Bangladesh as a political leader and reclaim her party, the Awami League. But she stands accused of ordering the police crackdown of July-August 2024, and the International Crimes Tribunal (ICT) in Dhaka has sentenced her to death for it You are logged in Loading... You don’t have any Active Subscription. Subscribed with another email? Logout and Login with that one. Your active subscription(s) Account subscription benefits alongside Premium Stories, Editorials, Opinions and more. Unlock these with Subscription Products you've access to Additional Subscription Benefits Account Settings Need help with your subscription? Updated- November 14, 2026 04:35 pm IST Former Bangladesh Prime Minister Sheikh Hasina. File | Photo Credit: Reuters The story so far:Sheikh Hasina sought a new passport and a national identity card from the Government of Bangladesh on Monday (November 9, 2026). In response, Acting Foreign Minister Humayun Kabir said that Bangladesh seeks her extradition under an “acceptable prisoner transfer process”. This is the latest episode in the Hasina saga that began when she came to India on August 5, 2024, after the student-led uprising toppled her government. Sheikh Hasina faces a dilemma. She wants to return as a political leader and reclaim her party, the Awami League. But she stands accused of ordering the police crackdown of July-August 2024 by the International Crimes Tribunal (ICT) in Dhaka, which has sentenced her to death for it. Her challenge is now to return to face justice and yet emerge as a leader of her country. She has refused to apologise for the crackdown, saying the police did not take her orders, but responded to angry agitators and militants who had provoked them. Published- Novem","Sat, 14 Nov 2026 16:35:52 +0530",https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/sheikh-hasinas-return-to-bangladesh-the-hurdles-the-risks-and-the-politics-explained/article71685349.ece,2026-11-15 03:30:56
